A||||1||laffer98||3.8 supercharged Swap||||||
Z||000000||laffer98||05-11-99||02:42 PM||||Can anyone out there give me some information on this swap.  How many hours actually go in to doing this type of swap.  Is there any specially labor required, cost, difficulties.  How happy are the people who have done one of these with their cars afterward... etc
Z||000001||Speedo||05-17-99||07:08 AM||||I've had the 3.8 supercharged engine installed in my 86 GT by a not to be mentioned company in Durango, Colorado, with less than satisfactory results.  Although touted as "the most bang for your buck" the performance and reliability of this particular installation has been less than what I expected.  A good deal of this is no doubt due to the people doing the installation.  After spending $8,000 for the engine swap, it has taken me over six months and $2,000 to fix the problems on the car after getting it back from Colorado, and this was supposed to be a "turn-key installation".  This weekend I will be taking it to a shop in Southern California to try to get the transmission problems sorted out.  After this is done it should be O.K.  My advise to you is to be VERY careful about doing this swap.  Unless you are really good at both mechanical and electrical systems (especially the electronics) get a professional to do the job.  Before you turn your car over to the pro, be sure you have talked to some of his customers to find out about the quality of his work.  Also - it won't be cheap.  
